A||N||16||ccollins||I'm new - Just bought Fiero over the weekend||||1||
Z||000000||ccollins||01-17-2001||12:29 PM||||Hi all.  I've been reading this forum for awhile as I was in the market for a fiero.  Just bought one over this past weekend ('87 GT) so now I can join in.<P>I have a few questions and will read any and all advise/comments that you care to pass on. <P>- Is there a way to manually open the headlights?  (Mine work but I'm wondering what happens some night when I get ready to go home and they don't pop up.)<P>- Do I really need to be cautiuos of engine fires and carry an extinguisher? <P>- I've read alot about the brakes getting hot.  Is there anything to that? <P>- On the interior center console back by the seats on each side there appears to be some kind of vents.  Can I put speakers in there? <P>- Do most of you do your own mechanical work or take your car to auto mechanics?  For those taking to mechanics do you have a hard time finding ones with Fiero experience? <P>- I think I need to replace the drivers side power window motor.  Anyone ever done that? How hard/expensive do you estimate that to be?  I had to replace one of these on my Bronco and it was tough..<P><BR>Sorry if any of these questions sound stupid, as I said I am a 4 day owner, give me a little time and I'll be an expert with the best of you. <P>-Charles<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||DrRodey||01-17-2001||12:39 PM||||Welcome to the forum and congratulations on your new car... when do we get pix?<P>ok now for your questions....<BR> -There is a round knob on each headlight motor, turning that will raise and lower the lights manually<BR>- Engine fires Really aren't any more of a problem in Fieros then in any other car. <BR>- don't know much bout the brakes ... sorry.<BR>- Those vents are there to provide fresh air and ventalation for the computer, DON"T PUT SPEAKERS THERE, unless you move the computer first.  THere are an infinate number of other places to put speakers.<BR>-Personally i do all the work on my car myself.<BR>-Working on anythign in the doors is a pain in the left nut. <P>There are no stupid questions.... only stupid answers.  We are all here to help.  Again welcome and Enjoy your Fiero.<P><P>------------------<BR>'88 Formula CONVERTABLE<BR>'85 Coupe<BR>AOL IM = drrodey<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/files/fieroPage.JPG">       <BR> <A HREF="http://freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/HomePlace.html" TARGET=_blank>freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/HomePlace.html</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||01-17-2001||12:43 PM||||Welcome to the Forum.<P>For headlights, open the front compartment and you'll see a round knob.  Turn the knob to manually bring up the headlights (40 turns or more, it's a pain) and carefully close the hood.  Check out <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A>  for headlight rebuild kits.<P>The vents in the center console are to help keep the ECM (computer) cool.  On long trips they can get warm, I wouldn't risk putting speakers there.<P>If you can find one at a parts store, grab a service manual for the Fiero.  Autozone has the Haynes manuals for about $12-$15.  I've found mine has come in handy a couple times.<P>Make sure the engine bay is free of leaves and no valve cover leaks.  I carry a fire extinguisher in my '84 mainly for grins because the '84's were more prone to fires.  It's always a good idea to have one with any car.  <P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Ta Ta For Now!<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue3.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 01-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||01-17-2001||12:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ccollins:<P>I have a few questions and will read any and all advise/comments that you care to pass on. <BR><B>Welcome to the forum. Today is my 1 year anniversary here, hope you stay around a while. I'll do my best to answer your questions.</B><P>- Is there a way to manually open the headlights?  (Mine work but I'm wondering what happens some night when I get ready to go home and they don't pop up.)<BR><B>Yes there is. If you look at the motor you will see the knob on the top saying "open" with an arrow.  By turning that knob you can raise the lights. CAUTION... if there is power to the motor, it will try to put itself down again, possibly putting an ouch on your fingers. Best bet is to disconnect the wires going to the motor before attempting. Look at it during the daytime to see what I'm talking about.</B><P>- Do I really need to be cautiuos of engine fires and carry an extinguisher? <BR><B>The hype about engine fires in Fieros is highly exaggerated, limited mostly to poorly maintained early 4cyl cars. That being said, I carry a fire extinquisher in my car, as I have in every car I've owned since a Toyota Corolla I owned went up in flames. It is a minor investment that can save you a lot of money, not to mention lives. I say get one.</B><BR> <BR>- I've read alot about the brakes getting hot.  Is there anything to that? <BR><B> The brakes on pre-88 are non-ventilated, so they are more prone to heat then ventilated discs. I have an 86 GT, but have never had any issues with brake fade from heat. If you do drive your car very aggressively, you may want to upgrade your brakes. Do a search of previous topics, there are lots of choices. But for average to mildly aggressive driving, I say they are fine. 14 year old brakelines do get a bit elastic though, you may want to upgrade to braided steel lines from the FieroStore.</B><P>- On the interior center console back by the seats on each side there appears to be some kind of vents.  Can I put speakers in there? <BR><B>Nope. these are cooling vents for the computer. Speakers would ot only hinder cooling, but the magnets may have an adverse effect on the computer. Plus, I don't think that they would make much of a difference in that location.</B><P>- Do most of you do your own mechanical work or take your car to auto mechanics?  For those taking to mechanics do you have a hard time finding ones with Fiero experience? <BR><B> Alas, many mechanics are faint of heart when it comes to working on Fieros. I think if you do find a mechanic that specializes in GM, you should be alright. But if you have a good spce to work on your car, and the tools and know-how, there are many things you should be able to do on your own. Always feel free to post questions in the Technical discussion area before you throw money at your problem.</B><P>- I think I need to replace the drivers side power window motor.  Anyone ever done that? How hard/expensive do you estimate that to be?  I had to replace one of these on my Bronco and it was tough..<BR><B>You got me on that one. I do know my motors are slow, but not bad enough to make me want to pull off my door panels. Check Ogre's cave (see link at bottom of page) I think he has some advice, if I remember correctly.</B><BR> <P><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000004||TheFiero64||01-17-2001||12:56 PM||||Welcome to the Forum and Fiero ownership.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000005||Carrolles||01-17-2001||02:15 PM||||Welcome to the forum and to the world of Fieros.<P>Here is a good site for info on Fieros.  There are many more out there.  Do a search for Fiero and you'll find plenty.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html</A> <P>Tell us about your car.<P>Welcome,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||01-17-2001||02:57 PM||||welcome!<P>a good trick to use to raise the lights, is to use an ink pen in one of the holes on top of the headlight motor... this raises it without a lot oftwisty turning on your hand.<P>do not put speakers in the center console!<P>i have been driving mine for 7 of the past 10 years and never had a fire.  in the last year, i have learned not to be afraid of working on a car, and started fixing things myself... it is much cheaper, and a lot of fun!<P>enjoy your car, and remember to post pics!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000007||ThatRickGuy||01-17-2001||03:33 PM||||Welcome to one of the greatest fiero sites ever!<P>1)Lights:<BR>Yes, one of my lights started doing the funky chicken a while ago. I got the parts from Rodney to fix it, but I've been putting it off. so, what I do is: standing outside the car w/ door open, hit the lights, run to front of car and slip fingers under light door, lift door. The light should come up on it's own. Eventually tho, even that will stop working and you'll have to do the rebuild tho. That, and it's negative mega cool points to have to do that on a date.<P>2)Fires:<BR>It's a simple thing about older cars... Check the oil!! I've seen many cars seize engines because the owners never checked the oil, just so happens fiero's have a habbit of throwing a rod when you run 'em hard w/o oil. The rod could then break threw the block, and start spewing oil on the insulation and exhaust... blam-o! fire! So yes, an estinguiser could be helpful, OR you could save youself the hassle and just check your oil ever time you get gas.<P>3)Brakes:<BR>Solid rotors... yes, but shouldn't be too bad unless you're autocrossing. save up some cash and do a brake upgrade.<P>4)Speakers:<BR>There is an option to put a sub infront of those vents, where the glove box is. someone was making an aftermarket deal on it, but I can't remember  where at the moment.<P>5)Mechanics:<BR>I do most of my own work. Except machining/building (can I really trust myself to torque everyting to exact specs, or pay a few extra buck so someone else can headache over it and give me a warrenty?) and I'm having a trusted mechanic friend take care of the lower ball joints, but that's not really fiero specific.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||sqoach||01-17-2001||05:10 PM||||well, all of your questions have been answered. i will add to the headlight part though. if a headlight won't go up, before you start to crank on that little wheel, disconnect the power. it should be a little blue connector. easy to see. unless the later model headlight motors don't have this little blue thingy. that's another thing, 84-86 headlight motors are different than 87-88 motors.<BR>about working on your car. most, but not all people i've taken it to hadn't worked on fieros. they're not too hard to work on in my opinion anyway. of course there's some exceptions. don't know about the window motors. haven't had to change one, yet.<BR>also, good place for speakers is in the factory locations. just get some good aftermarket speakers and it'll sound good in your car. and if you want some bass, try building a custom box for the passenger's side footwell. behind the seats is not the best spot because you will lose leg room. not comfortable. someone, however, did a fancy setup where they cut part of the firewall behind the seats to fit a couple of 12s i believe.<BR>anyway, i'm just rambling on.<P>welcome to the forum and have fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||01-17-2001||07:37 PM||||Before doing any brake upgrade go into the search function and check the tech section and the archived tech section. There are a number of issues with the brake upgrades that you need to understand BEFORE you chose and install one.||||reg||1
Z||000010||2birds||01-17-2001||08:20 PM||||Window motors are always slow for some reason.  They are standard to most GM cars for about a 20 year stretch, so they are not too pricey.  I paid $40 each at Discount Auto, but Autozone can probably beat that.  They are a PITA to replace, since you have to drill out some rivets, and then you get to realign the mechanism and glass.  I replaced the rivets with machine screws and nyloc nuts from the hardware store.  Might as well replace the dew wipes while your in there... ||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||01-17-2001||08:35 PM||||A word of advise about the headlight motors.  Defintiely disconnect the power before tinkering with those little wheels, and do not underestimate the power of that motor.  <P>I actually got my finger caught in the motor assembly after fixing a problem and if my dad had not been right there I might still be stuck there.  We had to unplug the motor and disassemble the thing to get my finger out.  It hurt for a long while.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000012||87MaroonGTKCMO||01-17-2001||10:24 PM||||Charles,<P>Congratulations on your new purchase. Can you tell us more about this Fiero? Where did you find this car? Miles? Auto or 5 spd?Color of interior and exterior? Condition?  Do you have any pics? Everyone here loves to see pictures.<P>Sean<BR>KCMO||||reg||5
Z||000013||ccollins||01-18-2001||10:43 AM||||Thanks for all the quick responses. I seem to have gotten my 1st round of questions answered. I'm sure I'll have more. <P>I currently don't have any pictures scanned. <P>Car is pretty much a stock '87 GT. It is the red/gray body color with tan interior. Automatic (really wanted a 5 speed, but couldn't find one) Car has 80k - seems to run OK so far.   I need to get my front tires, alignment, and suspension checked as it has somewhat of a shimmy at 70-80 mph.  Other than that no complaints, yet. <P>I used to have a Bertone X1/9 which I assume is where the idea of the Fiero's came from. It was a nice car also but was a little doggie with the little 4 cylinder it had in it.   I had to get rid of it when I started raising a family and needed more room. <P>Swore I would get either another X 1/9 or Fiero someday and finally was able to do so. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Songman||01-18-2001||11:15 AM||||I had an X 1/9 too... Cool little cars. Still love that Targa top! Amazingly, it was much better than my 911 Targa.. but my Corvette had them both beat in that area...<P>I always thought about getting another 911 too. The Fiero is a perfect reason not to get one! Not near the problem as with the Fiat's... Electrical was always my biggest problem. Couldn't run the radio, lights, windshield wipers, and heater at the same time... and forget about the other power accessories!<P>Welcome to the ranks of Fiero owner!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||6
Z||000015||ccollins||01-18-2001||11:26 AM||||I had a hard enough time finding parts for my X1/9 and that was 10 years ago so I would hate to think about the people that still have them trying to find parts today. <P>It was fun to drive, but I think my Fiero is going to be even better, most probably because of the V6 --vs- the 4cyl<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||sqoach||01-18-2001||01:12 PM||||hey now, the 4 cyl isn't that bad with a manual tranny. got me a 5 spd  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>you must post pics of your car soon or you will be kicked out!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>just kiddin  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
